  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Combine root sum and separation",
      "body": "Name the two roots. Use the quadratic coefficients to find their sum, then combine it with the given difference to obtain both roots.",
      "recap": "The root sum is the negative linear coefficient divided by the leading coefficient.",
      "pitfall": "A root’s distance from the midpoint is half the gap between the roots.",
      "pause": "Find the roots using their sum and difference.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "The root sum is the negative linear coefficient divided by the leading coefficient."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recover the constant from the product",
      "body": "Compare your two roots with the product relation for a quadratic. Retain the leading coefficient when recovering the constant term.",
      "recap": "For a quadratic, the root product is the constant coefficient divided by the leading coefficient.",
      "pitfall": "A nonmonic quadratic’s constant is not simply its root product.",
      "pause": "Calculate the root product and use it to determine the unknown constant.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "For a quadratic, the root product is the constant coefficient divided by the leading coefficient."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The root sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so their midpoint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Since they differ by 2, they are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>11</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The product of the roots is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>105</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Thus <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>105</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, option A.</strong></p><p>Equivalently, the quadratic formula gives root gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>121</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Setting that gap to 2 yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>121</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>, which gives the same value.</p>",
